  "full_response": "### **How a Hash Table Works**\nA **hash table** (also called a hash map) is a data structure that stores key-value pairs and provides efficient insertion, deletion, and lookup operations. It works by using a **hash function** to compute an index (or \"hash code\") into an array of **buckets** (or slots), where the desired value can be found.\n\n#### **Key Components:**\n1. **Hash Function**\n   - Takes a key as input and computes an integer (hash code) that determines the index in the underlying array.\n   - A good hash function:\n     - Distributes keys uniformly across buckets (minimizing collisions).\n     - Is deterministic (same key → same hash).\n     - Is fast to compute.\n\n2. **Buckets (Array of Slots)**\n   - Each bucket holds a key-value pair (or a linked list/other structure in case of collisions).\n\n3. **Collision Handling**\n   - **Separate Chaining**: Each bucket contains a linked list (or tree) of entries that hash to the same index.\n   - **Open Addressing**: If a collision occurs, the algorithm probes for the next available slot (e.g., linear probing, quadratic probing, or double hashing).\n\n---\n\n### **Operations & Time Complexity**\nThe average-case time complexity of hash table operations depends on the **load factor** (λ = number of entries / number of buckets). A good hash table keeps λ < 1 (often resizing when λ exceeds a threshold, e.g., 0.7).\n\n| **Operation**       | **Average Case** | **Worst Case**       | **Explanation**                                                                 |\n|----------------------|------------------|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Insertion**        | O(1)             | O(n)                 | Average: Hash function computes index in O(1). Worst: All keys collide.        |\n| **Deletion**         | O(1)             | O(n)                : Direct access via hash. Worst: All keys collide.                      |\n| **Lookup (Search)**  | O(1)             | O(n)                 | Average: Direct access via hash. Worst: All keys collide (degenerates to list).|\n| **Resizing**         | O(n) (amortized) | O(n)                 | When the table grows, rehashing all keys takes O(n) time (but happens rarely). ",
